Found while doing k87, reported rather than touched because the DSL is the
shared seam and two agents were live in the tree.

  perl -Ilib -MAPI::Docker::Type -e 'print API::Docker::Type->can("blessed")'
  -> true

lib/API/Docker/Type.pm imports blessed at line 11 and genuinely uses it at
line 255 in _is_type_tiny, so the import stays. What is missing is the
`use namespace::clean;` that closes the namespace afterwards.

This is the same shape as k82, one module over. Every role in the tree has
the line -- checked, all of them, including the six Role::Entity ones -- and
the DSL module does not.

Lower stakes than k82 was: API::Docker::Type is imported, not composed, so
the name sits on the DSL package itself rather than on 201 generated classes.
It is still a name in a namespace that should hold the keywords and nothing
else.

Same discipline as k82 asked for: check that nothing reaches it before
removing the leak, and check that namespace::clean does not take away
something the module's own subs still need -- _is_type_tiny calls blessed
after the clean would run.

Done in 36b0f68, one line. Verified after: API::Docker::Type answers neither ->blessed nor ->croak, while _is_type_tiny still calls blessed, all twelve croak sites still fire, use_module still loads lazily in both places, _isa_for still builds and rejects every shape, and both keywords plus the type vocabulary still arrive in a target package. The package went from 25 CODE symbols to 13 -- exactly the subs the file writes. Independently re-measured here: from_data with a nested field, a caller-data key untouched through TO_JSON, and the ambiguous-constructor croak all still work. No Changes entry: the names sat on the imported DSL package, never on a composed or inherited surface.
